{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785591528,"nonce":"JY8d2Qp37yH1hWpzG7uqLDPBLSE=","title":"Follow-up from 212bb13 stage 1 (the in-Pod drain helper). Stage 1 deliberately kept artifact harvest on its exec-tar leg: the artifact ObjectStore is control-plane-side with its own credentials, and a Pod must not hold them — so the exec framing machinery survives \"for artifacts only\".","message":"The honest end state, aligned with ADR-0063 (the Depot is the data-plane rendezvous — logs are already headed there): the helper POSTs artifacts to a Depot artifact route; the Depot streams them to the store with ITS credentials; the CP records metadata from the drain record. Then the last exec-tar dies, and exec_capture_stdout/framing/sentinel machinery goes with it.\n\nDecide when picking this up: who owns artifact credentials long-term (Depot as the single data-plane writer vs CP keeping them); size/rate bounds on the route (a fence token must not be an unbounded upload credential — cf. the /v1/drains ledger discipline); whether the artifact index annotation survives or the drain record carries it.\n\n- [ ] Artifacts leave the Pod via the Depot, not exec-tar\n- [ ] The Pod never holds object-store credentials\n- [ ] exec_capture_stdout + framing + sentinel machinery deleted (no remaining exec byte tunnels)\n- [ ] Fence-token upload bounded (size + count), stated in the route","files":null}]}